<!-- @diagram ecb7e8b069453bfd -->
### Background work, main-thread update

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" data-cols="2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Background thread</span><div class="node"><strong>Worker logic</strong><small>download, parse</small></div><div class="node"><strong><code>mainHandler.post { … }</code></strong><small>enqueue the UI update</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Main thread</span><div class="node"><strong>Message queue</strong></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>Looper pulls the task</strong></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Update UI</strong><small>then the loop repeats</small></div></div></div><figcaption>A main Handler is one way across; main-dispatcher coroutines and other UI scheduling APIs also marshal work to the UI thread. A background thread should not mutate views directly.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Misconceptions :: check -->
**Which thread runs it?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary><code>Handler(Looper.getMainLooper()).post { … }</code> called from a background thread.</summary>

The main thread. A Handler runs work on its Looper's thread, not the caller's.
</details>

<details><summary>Parsing a 10 MB JSON file posted to a <code>HandlerThread</code> that also handles camera frames.</summary>

That one thread: and while it parses, no camera frame is handled. Run CPU-heavy work on `Dispatchers.Default` or a pool.
</details>

<details><summary>A coroutine resumes on <code>Dispatchers.Main</code>.</summary>

The main thread. The regular Main dispatcher posts through its Handler; `Dispatchers.Main.immediate` can avoid an extra dispatch when already on the matching Looper.
</details>
